{
  "title": "China Rejects US 'Forced Labour' Tariffs, Calls for Cancellation",
  "publication_date": "2026-07-27T19:32:43+05:30",
  "summary": "Reuters reported that China rejected U.S. tariffs imposed on goods over alleged \"forced labour\" concerns and called for their cancellation. The move underscores ongoing tensions between Washington and Beijing over trade restrictions linked to human rights allegations and could deepen diplomatic and trade frictions.",
  "content": "Reuters reported that China has rejected U.S. tariffs imposed on certain imports on the basis of alleged \"forced labour\" and has called for those measures to be cancelled.\n\nThe statement, released alongside the report, framed the tariffs as unjustified and urged a reversal of the U.S. decision. Beijing described the measures as harmful to normal trade relations and called for dialogue to address the issue.\n\nThe dispute sits at the intersection of trade and human rights tensions between the United States and China. Washington has in recent years used import measures citing concerns about forced labour practices, while Beijing has consistently denied such allegations where they arise and criticized unilateral restrictions.\n\nThe Reuters dispatch did not provide further operational details about next steps or specific legal actions. Observers say the announcement is likely to add pressure to already strained bilateral ties and could prompt diplomatic engagement or formal challenges through international trade bodies.\n\nReporting credited Reuters; the item reflects the headline and brief reporting as published on the stated date.",
